{"id":"u-44faed29-b766-4439-9a81-dcf00395a4a5","src":"community","title":"Limitbreaker V2.0- Adapted from Colton Engelbrecht","author":"Hayden S.","url":"https://www.boostcamp.app/users/4mNsv1-haydos-12-week-complete-strength-builder","desc":"Limit Breaker V2 — Top Single Edition\nThree phases, four to five days per week, built around a single design principle: a near-maximal single before every primary working block on squat, bench, and deadlift. The single isn't the workout — it's the primer. A heavy RPE 7–9 effort activates high-threshold motor units and elevates neural drive so the working sets that follow are faster and more technically accessible at the same load. The single takes the edge off the bar. The working sets build the strength.\nLoading System\nAll weights are expressed as a percentage of estimated 1RM. Working sets run +4.5% per week across the block. Top singles follow an RPE arc of 7 → 8 → 8.5 → 9 across each four-week phase, then reset. The reset is nominal — because each phase uses lower rep ranges and the athlete is objectively stronger, the absolute load at RPE 7 is higher each time. Working sets are prescribed at −3% below standard RPE chart values to offset the fatigue cost of the preceding single.\nPhase 1 — Development (Weeks 1–4)\n68–81.5% working sets. Rep ranges of 3×8 tapering to 3×6. High volume builds work capacity and movement quality. Accessories performed at 0–1 RIR throughout.\nPhase 2 — Strength (Weeks 5–8)\n77–90.5% working sets. Rep ranges of 3×5 tapering to 3×3. Maximal force output is the target. The phase opens heavier in absolute terms than Development despite resetting to RPE 7.\nPhase 3 — Peak (Weeks 9–12, revised)\nCompetition preparation. Volume strips aggressively. Close grip bench and paused squats are removed. Weeks 3–4 are fully autoregulated. The top single escalates to RPE 9; back-off sets drop to submaximal doubles at 77–80% — following the RTS inverse taper model where the single gets harder and the back-off gets lighter. This is not two near-max singles. The back-off is a pattern reinforce, not a second max effort. Long pause bench is capped at RPE 8 throughout the peak to prevent excessive neurological demand on the bench specifically. Accessories are stripped to maintenance volume at 2–3 RIR; curls, isolation work, and redundant movements are cut entirely. Day 3 is cut pre-competition.\nDeload\nOptional between blocks. Strongly recommended within 14 days of competition. Main lift top singles drop to RPE 6, back-off sets to 60%. Accessories match the stripped peak structure or are cut entirely pre-meet.\nDay 4 — Long Pause Bench\nMandatory across all three phases. A two-second paused bench variation run on its own dedicated day. Develops bottom-position strength and pause confidence that transfers directly to competition bench. Loads are RPE-governed rather than percentage-based.","level":["Intermediate","Advanced"],"goals":["Strength","Muscle"],"equipment":["Full Gym"],"minutes":120,"joins":16,"rate":0,"rateCount":1,"updated":"2026-10-20T20:50:56.960Z","variations":[{"name":"default","days":["Day 1","Day 2","Day 3","Day 4","Day 5","Day 6","Day 7"],"weeks":[[{"ex":[{"n":"Bench Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":1,"pct":92}]},{"n":"Bench Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"pct":68,"x":3}]},{"n":"Bench Press (Close Grip)","sets":[{"r":8,"rpe":8,"x":3}]},{"n":"Incline Bench Press (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"r":12,"rpe":9.5,"x":3}]},{"n":"Hammer Curl (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"rpe":9.5,"x":3}]},{"n":"Lateral Raise (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"r":12,"rpe":9.5,"x":3}]},{"n":"Bicep Curl (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"rpe":9.5,"x":3}]},{"n":"Seated Overhead Extension (EZ Bar)","sets":[{"r":8,"rpe":9.5,"x":3}]}]},{"ex":[{"n":"Squat (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":1,"pct":92}]},{"n":"Squat (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"pct":68,"x":3}]},{"n":"Squat (Paused)","sets":[{"r":6,"rpe":8,"x":2}]},{"n":"Leg Press","sets":[{"r":12,"rpe":9.5,"x":3}]},{"n":"Leg Extension","sets":[{"r":10,"rpe":9.5,"x":3}]},{"n":"Bench Press